course: dictionary
lesson: 12793
title: Time that elapses
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Elapse is a formal verb, and it is used for time. When time elapses, it passes by. «Ten minutes elapsed before the bus arrived.» The clue is "before the bus arrived": time went by while people waited.
explain: The past form is elapsed, and you will meet it in maths. Elapsed time is how much time has passed between a start and an end. «The film started at 2 o'clock and ended at 3:30, so 1 hour and 30 minutes elapsed.» Count the time from the start to the end.
explain: Use the swap check. Put "passed by" into the sentence: «Ten minutes passed by before the bus arrived» still makes sense, so passed by is the right meaning.
fact: Elapse means to pass by, and it is used for time.
fact: «Elapsed» is the past form: «Three days had elapsed since the storm.»
fact: Elapsed time is the time between a start and an end, such as «The race started at 9:00 and ended at 9:45, so 45 minutes elapsed.»
fact: The phrase «time elapsed» means the same as elapsed time, and it is common in exam questions.
fact: Elapse is a formal word, so you will see it more in書 stories, reports and exams than in everyday talk.
exercise: «Ten minutes elapsed before the bus arrived.» What does elapsed mean here? | Arrived early | Was cancelled | *Passed by | Got lost
exercise: A film starts at 2:00 and ends at 3:30. What is the elapsed time? | 3 hours 30 minutes | 2 hours | 30 minutes | *1 hour 30 minutes
exercise: Which phrase means the time that has passed between a start and an end? | *Elapsed time | Spare time | Early time | Lost time
test: «As the years elapsed, the little oak grew into a giant.» What does elapsed mean? | Stopped | Grew smaller | Fell down | *Passed
test: Mia says elapse means "to end". Is she right? | No, it means jump | Yes, it means start | *No, it means time passing by | Yes, it means finish
exam: Sam's swim started at 10:15 and finished at 10:45. How much time elapsed? | 45 minutes | *30 minutes | 15 minutes | 25 minutes
